  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nCluster of storms near Decatur will continue to move east\nthrough the late afternoon and evening hours. HRRR model has\nfinally caught onto it and based on the continued cold cloud\ntops on satellite and unstable airmass out ahead of it (SBCAPE \nover 4000 and MLCAPE over 2500) believe this cluster will keep\ngoing and will produce scattered wind damage. \n\nBesides the ongoing cluster of storms, scattered showers and \nstorms will remain possible late this afternoon into this \nevening as a frontal system drops through the CWA. Winds have \nalready begun to turn northwest over parts of northern IL and\nthat will continue as the front moves south. Airmass behind the\ncurrent cluster has stabilized some and think that chances of\nconvection in west central IL may not be as high. Current\nmesoscale analysis suggests best instability remains in eastern\nIL and this could get scoured out as well once this cluster\nmoves into IN. Though airmass does not look favorable at the\nmoment, some recover is still possible with all the cloud-free\nskies back in MO. So will keep evening chance pops over most of\nthe area with only likely pops south of a Springfield to Paris\nline. If recovery does not occur, these could be pulled back.\nThe evening convection is expected to develop in west central\nIllinois as a remnant MCV from northwest MO move into the area\nand interacts with the front moving south. This convection is\nexpected to move east as well and affect areas where the SPC\nslight risk is located at. This reasoning is the support for the\nhigher pops in the forecast, mentioned earlier. \n\nA brief break in the active weather and the heat looks likely \nfor Monday into Tuesday. A high pressure center will set up over\nthe Upper Mississippi Valley/Great Lakes region behind a front \nthat is currently moving through IL. Temperatures starting \nMonday into much of the next week will be mainly in the upper \n70s to 80s for highs and 60s to low 70s for lows.\n\nMore showers and storms re-enter the area for Tuesday evening \ninto Wednesday night. SPC has a marginal risk for severe \nweather for Tuesday night west of I-55, with damaging winds the\nmost likely threat. There are additional chances for rain \nthrough Thursday before returning to a mostly dry forecast into\nthe late week.",
